00:02:40.208 --> 00:02:42.291
Andy gave a little
bit of his background

00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:45.708
and his career trajectory
that led him to a fund

00:02:45.708 --> 00:02:47.250
that would invest
in his company,

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.708
and you have a really
interesting career trajectory,

00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.959
which maybe you could start--

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:56.083
- I think I should get Gary to
invest in my company either.

00:02:56.083 --> 00:02:57.125
- Did you try?

00:02:57.125 --> 00:02:58.458
- Of course I tried.

00:02:58.458 --> 00:02:59.625
- Why did you--

00:02:59.625 --> 00:03:00.542
- I don't recall.

00:03:00.542 --> 00:03:02.166
Let me ask you a quick question.
(laughing)

00:03:02.166 --> 00:03:03.834
- You don't recall because
it didn't even register?

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:04.959
- No, I wanna get back
to that in a minute.

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:06.417
But there was something
interesting that just happened.

00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:10.542
Did you ask him, would he
invest in the person, who,

00:03:10.542 --> 00:03:13.417
if he took the term deal,
which is the early deal, right?

00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:14.250
- Yeah.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.959
- Well the interesting
part there is

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.834
that he would have not
known the other outcome.

00:03:20.834 --> 00:03:21.542
- Right.

00:03:21.542 --> 00:03:22.291
- Like if they
took that first deal,

00:03:22.291 --> 00:03:23.917
they just wouldn't know.
- Right.

00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:26.792
- So I think that's something
I'm always fascinated by,

00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:29.333
which is people are so
crippled by decisions,

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:31.708
there's just no way to know
what would have happened,

00:03:31.708 --> 00:03:34.000
you know, at the time
you're making the decision.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.166
So much of this is Monday
morning quarterbacking.

00:03:37.166 --> 00:03:38.083
Fascinating.

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.959
So anyway, none the less.

00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:42.208
- Let's move over to the middle.
- Yeah.

00:03:42.208 --> 00:03:44.500
(laughing)

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.083
I've known this man since
I was 14, 15 years old,

00:03:48.083 --> 00:03:50.583
because my family
had a liquor store

00:03:50.583 --> 00:03:52.041
in Springfield, New Jersey,

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:55.083
that I started working
at when I was 14,

00:03:55.083 --> 00:03:57.750
that I later turned into
something called Wine Library,

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.625
and launched one of
the first three or four

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:01.500
e-commerce wine
businesses in America,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.542
called WineLibrary.com
back in 1996,

00:04:04.542 --> 00:04:08.959
