against something

00:06:14.375 --> 00:06:16.250
more financially interesting.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.250
So when I read the article
about YouTube's sale,

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.834
there was one
article that featured

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:24.166
Ron Conway's angel investor,
investment,

00:06:24.166 --> 00:06:26.333
and I said what the
fuck's an angel investor?

00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:27.417
I googled it.

00:06:27.417 --> 00:06:28.875
(laughing)

00:06:28.875 --> 00:06:31.708
Within 20 minutes I decided
I would be an angel investor.

00:06:31.708 --> 00:06:32.458
(laughing)

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:34.875
And went to South by
Southwest three months later,

00:06:34.875 --> 00:06:37.208
became friends with
Ev Williams and Blaine Cook,

00:06:37.208 --> 00:06:38.583
the original CTO,

00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:41.625
and I think your firm at
the time, me and Kevin Rose,

00:06:41.625 --> 00:06:44.500
bought up Blaine's
original stock of Twitter,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.083
so I bought a ton of Twitter,
real cheap.

00:06:47.083 --> 00:06:48.542
I didn't think of myself
as an angel investor,

00:06:48.542 --> 00:06:50.000
I just thought that
was a smart decision

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.417
because I thought
Twitter would be big.

00:06:51.417 --> 00:06:53.208
Then I went home f
rom South by, made a--

00:06:53.208 --> 00:06:54.041
- [Ed] Profit, by the way?

00:06:54.041 --> 00:06:56.125
- Well it did for me
because I sold my shares,

00:06:56.125 --> 00:06:57.291
so I did good.

00:06:57.291 --> 00:07:00.083
(laughing)

00:07:00.083 --> 00:07:02.542
Came home, made a video,
I was making wine videos,

00:07:02.542 --> 00:07:03.542
came home, made a
video that said

00:07:03.542 --> 00:07:05.917
Facebook should be
worried about Twitter.

00:07:05.917 --> 00:07:07.708
There was a guy who was head
of platform at the time,

00:07:07.708 --> 00:07:08.792
at Facebook, that I didn't know,

00:07:08.792 --> 00:07:10.708
by the name of
Dave Morin, sent me an e-mail

00:07:10.708 --> 00:07:13.041
like, "Hey, this video
has gotten really viral

00:07:13.041 --> 00:07:15.667
"inside of Facebook, would
you come out to Palo Alto

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:16.959
"and give a talk?"

00:07:16.959 --> 00:07:19.834
So I came to room like this,
in front of the whole company,

00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:21.708
it was only 400 employees,

00:07:21.708 --> 00:07:24.250
and talked about
consumer behavior

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.083
and why I thought the
world was changing.

00:07:26.083 --> 00:07:27.375
That caught Mark's attention,

00:07:27.375 --> 00:07:28.583
we went out to
dinner that night,

00:07:28.583 --> 00:07:31.333
and a week later I bought a
lot of stock from his parents,

00:07:31.333 --> 00:07:34.083
and then somehow I got
David Karp to reopen

00:07:34.083 --> 00:07:36.041
the B round of Tumblr,

00:07:36.041 --> 00:07:37.583
by the way stick with me here,

00:07:37.583 --> 00:07:41.125
the B round at a
$14 million valuation,

00:07:41.125 --> 00:07:42.583
and I made an
investment into Tumblr.

00:07:42.583 --> 00:07:44.750
So the first three things
I invested in were Twitter,

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.166
Facebook, and Tumblr.

00:07:46.166 --> 00:07:48.333
(laughing)

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:50.375
Yeah, it was when I was smart,

00:07:50.375 --> 00:07:51.750
I was making bets on things

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:53.250
that I thought would
be successful.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.083
I didn't think of
myself as an investor.

00:07:55.083 --> 00:07:57.166
I got progressively
stupider after that.

00:07:57.166 --> 00:07:58.959
(laughing)

00:07:58.959 --> 00:08:00.333
- [Ed] Why do you
think that was?

00:08:00.333 --> 00:08:02.375
- Because I started
trying to be an investor.

00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:05.041
I literally went from
being, I'm a businessman,

00:08:05.041 --> 00:08:06.875
these are good businesses,
this is good.

00:08:06.875 --> 00:08:08.125
The market changed.

00:08:08.125 --> 00:08:10.041
I mean this is
Tumblr B round 2008.

00:08:10.041 --> 00:08:11.792
B round 14 million.

00:08:11.792 --> 00:08:13.750
Now every fake,
bullshit entrepreneur

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:17.083
running around this campus wants
seven million, four million

00:08:17.083 --> 00:08:19.458
for their bullshit idea
that they have no prayer on,

00:08:19.458 --> 00:08:21.542
because entrepreneurship
is popular,

00:08:21.542 --> 00:08:23.125
and instead of going
to Bain or McKinsey,

00:08:23.125 --> 00:08:24.500
they think they can
start a fucking app

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:25.959
that people are gonna use.

00:08:25.959 --> 00:08:27.917
(laughing)

00:08:27.917 --> 00:08:30.125
(clapping)

00:08:31.458 --> 00:08:33.875
- [Ed] Those people
who are clapping,

00:08:33.875 --> 00:08:36.083
are you clapping because
he just described you?

00:08:36.083 --> 00:08:38.542
(laughing)

00:08:38.542 --> 00:08:41.208
- Ed, they're always clapping
and laughing when I speak

00:08:41.208 --> 00:08:42.917
because it's the truth,
we all know it.

00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:45.917
This is the greatest era of
fake entrepreneurship ever,

00:08:45.917 --> 00:08:48.250
which makes it challenging
for anybody here

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.041
that wants to go
into venture investing.

00:08:50.041 --> 00:08:53.708
I barely created one
investment for myself last year

00:08:53.708 --> 00:08:56.542
'cause I'm so scared of
the early stage ecosystem,

00:08:56.542 --> 00:08:58.333
because the supply
and demand curve

00:08:58.333 --> 00:09:01.041
of actual entrepreneurs that
can see these things through,

00:09:01.041 --> 00:09:03.250
versus everyone who
fancies themselves in that,

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:04.583
has broke.

00:09:04.583 --> 00:09:08.000
And so I'm just desperately,

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.417
I don't understand macro,
global economics well enough

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:15.166
to understand who and why this
is being held up for so long,

00:09:15.166 --> 00:09:16.625
but I'm just desperately waiting

00:09:16.625 --> 00:09:18.542
for this whole
fucking thing to crash.

00:09:18.542 --> 00:09:19.583
(laughter)

00:09:19.583 --> 00:09:21.708
- But here's the interesting
thing to me is that

00:09:21.708 --> 00:09:24.500
at a certain point, you just
decided you were gonna be

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.500
an angel investor, you didn't
know what it was, right?

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.625
- That's right.
- So you just kinda like,

00:09:28.625 --> 00:09:30.834
you just decided that's
what you were gonna be.

00:09:30.834 --> 00:09:33.375
- I thought there was a moment
in time where the internet

00:09:33.375 --> 00:09:35.500
that I grew up in, built
my dad's liquor store

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.583
was starting to hit true
scale and consumer behavior

00:09:38.583 --> 00:09:40.458
was about to change for real.

00:09:40.458 --> 00:09:43.041
And so things like
Birchbox and Uber

00:09:43.041 --> 00:09:44.417
and other things I did well with

00:09:44.417 --> 00:09:48.792
they were all still, back in
2007 to 2010-11 time frame

00:09:50.083 --> 00:09:52.792
where valuations were a little

00:09:52.792 --> 00:09:56.000
not more fair, but I would
tell you that we were all

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.166
I was very early stage,
we were still dealing

00:09:58.166 --> 00:10:01.041
with four million dollar
idea stage, the difference is

00:10:01.041 --> 00:10:02.959
the entrepreneurs that were
building those companies

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:06.250
in 2007, eight, nine,
10, they couldn't breathe

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:07.834
doing anything else.

00:10:07.834 --> 00:10:08.792
There was no--

00:10:08.792 --> 00:10:11.458
- They didn't think they were
going to get rich doing it.

00:10:11.458 --> 00:10:14.291
- Some of them, I think some,
especially the early part.

00:10:14.291 --> 00:10:16.208
My South-By 2007

00:10:16.208 --> 00:10:18.083
experience was wild.

00:10:18.083 --> 00:10:19.500
I went there.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.125
I said something about business

00:10:22.125 --> 00:10:23.708
and everybody looked at
me like I was the worst

00:10:23.708 --> 00:10:25.959
piece of shit they'd ever seen.

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:27.667
This Web 2.0 movement

00:10:29.708 --> 00:10:31.834
Growing from the ashes
of the first Web bubble,

00:10:31.834 --> 00:10:34.875
these were people that wanted
to change the world for real.

00:10:34.875 --> 00:10:36.542
I just wanted to sell

00:10:36.542 --> 00:10:39.166
more Peter Michael Les Pavots.

00:10:39.166 --> 00:10:40.000
That's for you.
- [Ed] That's a wine.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.417
- That was for you.

00:10:41.417 --> 00:10:44.583
So, yeah, that's true, but they
were also true entrepreneurs

00:10:44.583 --> 00:10:48.083
every day, I meet, every
month I meet with 15 people

00:10:48.083 --> 00:10:51.291
that 10 years ago, were
gonna be consultants

00:10:51.291 --> 00:10:55.375
and bankers that now think
the Uber of dry cleaning

00:10:55.375 --> 00:10:56.750
is their way out.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.000
- But how do you evaluate
that? How do you evaluate

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.750
that person now, compared
to what you called

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.000
were the true believers?

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.625
- So, it really is interesting.

00:11:04.625 --> 00:11:07.417
I've really gravitated
later and later stage

00:11:07.417 --> 00:11:09.500
because I realized
my marketing talent

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:11.708
and what I was good at
over the last decade

00:11:11.708 --> 00:11:13.917
is actually far better
used for things that have

00:11:13.917 --> 00:11:16.083
a little maturity to them,
where I can use

00:11:16.083 --> 00:11:18.667
my arbitrage of
marketing capabilities.

00:11:18.667 --> 00:11:22.208
So, I've become less interested
in the very early stage

00:11:22.208 --> 00:11:25.000
because the only thing I now
eval--, if I'm going to make

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.125
an angel investment pure
idea phase, I need to know

00:11:28.125 --> 00:11:31.417
that that person's,
my 100% evaluation now

00:11:31.417 --> 00:11:32.875
is on the person

00:11:32.875 --> 00:11:34.500
could care less about the idea

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.000
and I need to know
if they sold Blow-Pops

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.000
in junior high.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.834
Like, I'm not kidding, like.

00:11:39.834 --> 00:11:41.250
- [Ed] When you say
Blow Pops, it doesn't matter

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.000
if they sold Blow Pops
as long as they started

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:43.875
some business.

00:11:43.875 --> 00:11:45.417
- Zuck sold, like, like he

00:11:45.417 --> 00:11:47.625
like put music on CD's
and sold them in cla--

00:11:47.625 --> 00:11:49.959
Like, that's salesmanship DNA,

00:11:49.959 --> 00:11:51.458
that grit,

00:11:51.458 --> 00:11:53.917
that I can't breathe
without selling thing

00:11:53.917 --> 00:11:56.583
is very needed because
the other problem we have

00:11:56.583 --> 00:11:58.583
in the ecosystem and
we deal with this

00:11:58.583 --> 00:12:02.291
is we also have a crazy
reaction to failure.

00:12:03.583 --> 00:12:05.708
I've been at too
many dinners where

00:12:05.708 --> 00:12:08.333
the dinner is,
I went out of business

00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:11.208
and everybody sits around
the table in a circle jerk

00:12:11.208 --> 00:12:13.458
of oh, it's great
that you learned.

00:12:13.458 --> 00:12:15.667
(laughter)

00:12:16.708 --> 00:12:19.625
- [Ed] And your view is
it sucks that you learned?

00:12:19.625 --> 00:12:22.875
- My view is like, fuck,
I don't wanna lose my money.

00:12:22.875 --> 00:12:25.208
And, like, I definitely
don't wanna buy you dinner

00:12:25.208 --> 00:12:27.417
and make you feel
good that you suck.

00:12:27.417 --> 00:12:29.708
(laughter)

00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:32.208
But, but, right?

00:12:32.208 --> 00:12:35.000
I mean we have an
unbelievably awesome culture

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.834
that has a unique twist to it.

00:12:36.834 --> 00:12:38.083
Like, I don't know, like

00:12:38.083 --> 00:12:40.417
it feels very weird that
I've worked very hard

00:12:40.417 --> 00:12:41.625
my whole fucking life

00:12:41.625 --> 00:12:43.375
wrote a $250,000 check.

00:12:43.375 --> 00:12:45.125
The person was skiing,

00:12:45.125 --> 00:12:46.166
skiing,

00:12:46.166 --> 00:12:49.291
four months before his
company went outta business

00:12:49.291 --> 00:12:50.583
and then I'm supposed
to go to a dinner

00:12:50.583 --> 00:12:52.834
and celebrate the learnings
he had in three years?

00:12:52.834 --> 00:12:53.875
That's broken.

00:12:53.875 --> 00:12:56.625
That's the softness of
our current culture.

00:12:56.625 --> 00:12:58.166
I genuinely believe that.

00:12:58.166 --> 00:13:01.417
- [Ed] And do you think
that's your view because

00:13:01.417 --> 00:13:03.208
does that relate back
to your upbringing?

00:13:03.208 --> 00:13:04.041
- Sure.

00:13:05.166 --> 00:13:06.917
I think it's a hell of
a lot easier to feel

00:13:06.917 --> 00:13:09.166
the way I feel that,
because I walked K-Mart

00:13:09.166 --> 00:13:11.625
when I was seven and split
toilet paper with my mom

00:13:11.625 --> 00:13:16.083
versus going to fucking
Horace Mann in Columbia.

00:13:16.083 --> 00:13:17.667
Yes.

00:13:17.667 --> 00:13:19.583
- But Zuck didn't
grow up that way.

00:13:19.583 --> 00:13:21.083
- Oh, by the way, I'm
talking 'bout me personally.

00:13:21.083 --> 00:13:22.708
I don't think my
path is the only path.

00:13:22.708 --> 00:13:24.417
I think there's
killers that went to

00:13:24.417 --> 00:13:26.250
Horace Mann in Columbia.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:27.500
But if you're asking
me the question

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:28.708
yes, I think that

00:13:28.708 --> 00:13:30.208
my reaction to that--
- [Ed] By killers you mean

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:31.667
homicidal killers?

00:13:31.667 --> 00:13:32.667
- Nah.
(laughter)

00:13:32.667 --> 00:13:34.583
I mean the slang
term for real executors.

00:13:34.583 --> 00:13:35.333
Here's what I know.

00:13:35.333 --> 00:13:36.917
The balance of

00:13:36.917 --> 00:13:39.000
this ecosystem, I don't
know what the class

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.458
wants to do, whether
build their companies

00:13:40.458 --> 00:13:41.708
or go and invest.

00:13:41.708 --> 00:13:44.625
as it currently sits right
now, it's never been tougher

00:13:44.625 --> 00:13:48.166
at the earliest stage,
because you have just so

00:13:48.166 --> 00:13:51.458
many pretenders, and
the truth is it's hard.

00:13:51.458 --> 00:13:53.166
It's hard to like, figure them.

00:13:53.166 --> 00:13:56.834
I can ask you about putting
music on CD's but like

00:13:56.834 --> 00:13:58.834
that's only one
little tiny data point

00:13:58.834 --> 00:13:59.667
You're really--

00:13:59.667 --> 00:14:01.750
- But the number of
pretenders you have

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.875
is correlated with the rise
in the dollars available

00:14:04.875 --> 00:14:05.750
to those pretenders--

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.583
- And the romance and branding
of being an entrepreneur.

00:14:09.583 --> 00:14:12.792
The brand equity around
being an entrepreneur today

00:14:12.792 --> 00:14:14.750
is stunning.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:17.500
It is stunning for me
to, in the world like

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:19.959
I can't believe how cool it is

00:14:19.959 --> 00:14:22.417
in real life to be an
entrepreneur.

00:14:22.417 --> 00:14:24.834
And that makes a
lot of young individuals

00:14:24.834 --> 00:14:27.458
gravitate towards that.

00:14:27.458 --> 00:14:29.542
- But you're suggesting overall

00:14:29.542 --> 00:14:31.625
that's worse than if
those young individuals

00:14:31.625 --> 00:14:33.708
went to work for
Goldman Sachs or McKinsey

00:14:33.708 --> 00:14:35.458
- I am, 'cause I'll
tell what we are not--

00:14:35.458 --> 00:14:37.583
- You think the world
is a better place

00:14:37.583 --> 00:14:39.708
even with the bad ideas?

00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:40.667
- No, I do not.

00:14:40.708 --> 00:14:42.083
- And getting funded by
- And let me explain why.

00:14:42.083 --> 00:14:44.333
The biggest conversation
that I'm fascinated by

00:14:44.333 --> 00:14:47.750
is the suicide rate
at Wharton last year.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.333
So we have, in parallel

00:14:50.333 --> 00:14:52.917
a narrative that
anybody can do it.

00:14:52.917 --> 00:14:55.667
And everybody's entitled
to win in entrepreneurship.

00:14:55.667 --> 00:14:57.500
And there's an
underbelly in our world

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.500
that I'm concerned about,
and you know we've talked

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.708
about this in a different panel

00:15:00.708 --> 00:15:03.375
This is a big thing, I mean like

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.708
there's certain individuals
who've been winning

00:15:06.708 --> 00:15:09.041
in the first
20 years of their life

00:15:09.041 --> 00:15:11.583
in a game that was fixed
before they played it.

00:15:11.583 --> 00:15:13.125
And then they go into the market

00:15:13.125 --> 00:15:15.166
and the market
punches them in the mouth

00:15:15.166 --> 00:15:16.333
and they don't know how to react

00:15:16.333 --> 00:15:17.750
and so yes, I do think

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:21.000
for the cliched picture
that I'm painting right now

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.667
that there are certain
individuals that I just

00:15:22.667 --> 00:15:24.667
genuinely believe would
have had a better life

00:15:24.667 --> 00:15:27.250
being the number
seven at Facebook

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.875
instead of the
number one of Shitbook.

00:15:30.875 --> 00:15:32.667
(laughter)

00:15:32.667 --> 00:15:34.250
Or, or, or

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.458
making 230,000 a year

00:15:36.458 --> 00:15:39.542
on Wall Street or Consulting
Land at 29 years old.

00:15:39.542 --> 00:15:41.834
Yes, I think there's a lot of
people that would be better.

00:15:41.834 --> 00:15:43.792
Like, I love
entrepreneurship the most

00:15:43.792 --> 00:15:47.250
but nothing works when
it's forced or off-balance.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:48.083
Nothing.

00:15:48.083 --> 00:15:49.417
- [Ed] And Andy your
point is just like

00:15:49.417 --> 00:15:51.208
do anything other than

00:15:51.208 --> 00:15:52.208
Goldman's?

00:15:52.208 --> 00:15:54.083
- Yeah, sort of, right,
in other words--

00:15:54.083 --> 00:15:56.000
- Unless you're built
for Goldman--

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:57.291
- But what does
that mean, right?

00:15:57.291 --> 00:15:58.250
- Well I know one thing.

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:00.125
Not everybody's built
to be an entrepreneur.

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:01.458
- No, that's right.
- That's insane.

00:16:01.458 --> 00:16:03.500
- Right, but it works
the same way too, right.

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:05.917
In other words,
it used to be, right,

00:16:05.917 --> 00:16:08.500
you graduate school and
then you go work for a bank

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:09.875
or a law firm or
you go into medicine.

00:16:09.875 --> 00:16:10.875
Those were a
bunch of different paths.

00:16:10.875 --> 00:16:13.417
- Which all suck if, for me.

00:16:13.417 --> 00:16:15.708
- And they may, but some
people still do that, right?

00:16:15.708 --> 00:16:16.542
- Yes.

00:16:16.542 --> 00:16:18.542
- Entrepreneurship is
about being empowered

00:16:18.542 --> 00:16:20.291
to take control of
your professional life.

00:16:20.291 --> 00:16:21.208
- Okay.

00:16:21.208 --> 00:16:22.041
- Right, at some level.

00:16:22.041 --> 00:16:24.166
- Do you know how many
people are not capable of that?

00:16:24.166 --> 00:16:26.917
Do you know how hard
entrepreneurship is?

00:16:26.917 --> 00:16:29.667
- But that's the market though,
that's called the market.

00:16:29.667 --> 00:16:31.125
It won't work for everyone.

00:16:31.125 --> 00:16:32.417
Not everyone that goes
to work at McKinsey

00:16:32.417 --> 00:16:33.834
or Goldman Sachs is
gonna be successful.

00:16:33.834 --> 00:16:35.750
- That's like literally
telling a basketball player

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.000
that you should
become a hockey player.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:38.834
You know that, right?

00:16:38.834 --> 00:16:41.166
Like, like self-awareness
is very important.

00:16:41.166 --> 00:16:43.000
And something that
should be debated.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:44.750
And I think we're forcing
too many people into

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.166
the entrepreneurial
track that do not have

00:16:47.166 --> 00:16:48.875
the stomach to be an
entrepreneur.

00:16:48.875 --> 00:16:50.708
- When you say
we're forcing meaning

00:16:50.708 --> 00:16:53.125
we are, or Ed is, or who is?

00:16:53.125 --> 00:16:55.500
- All of us combined,
the ecosystem

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:57.583
the self-promotion within itself

00:16:57.583 --> 00:16:59.750
the outside world
that decided it was

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.542
the cool thing to
talk about for a decade.

00:17:01.542 --> 00:17:03.291
These pendulums swing.

00:17:03.291 --> 00:17:05.625
But what I'm most worried
about, and I refer to it

00:17:05.625 --> 00:17:07.792
'cause I believe that this
room all understand it

00:17:07.792 --> 00:17:08.708
better than me

00:17:08.708 --> 00:17:09.875
I'm dying to know

00:17:09.875 --> 00:17:11.959
why it can still stay up.

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:14.000
Like there's so much
money in the system.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:15.917
There's so much
other stuff going on and

00:17:15.917 --> 00:17:18.542
government involvement and
so many other variables

00:17:18.542 --> 00:17:21.083
that are playing out that
are just above my pay grade

00:17:21.083 --> 00:17:23.333
that I'm fascinated
by when they cripple

00:17:23.333 --> 00:17:26.083
because this much fakeness

00:17:26.083 --> 00:17:27.750
has an inevitable fate.

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:29.583
- [Ed] So why
are you raising a fund?

00:17:29.583 --> 00:17:31.208
- I'm raising a fund
because I'm going

00:17:31.208 --> 00:17:34.250
Series B and Series C where
I think my marketing machine

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:35.542
that I've spent--so

00:17:35.542 --> 00:17:37.250
my behavior, as you guys know

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:38.875
over the last five, six years

00:17:38.875 --> 00:17:41.291
instead of where I had a lot
of leverage to raise capital

00:17:41.291 --> 00:17:42.333
and do my thing

00:17:42.333 --> 00:17:45.458
I took a major,
on paper, step back

00:17:45.458 --> 00:17:49.166
and built a client service
digital social agency

00:17:49.166 --> 00:17:51.000
that I've grown in
the last five years

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.208
from zero to
100 million in revenue

00:17:53.208 --> 00:17:54.041
right?

00:17:54.041 --> 00:17:57.125
To give me leverage for
when the shit hits the fan.

00:17:57.125 --> 00:17:59.917
And so, what I want to do with
the fund in the short term

00:17:59.917 --> 00:18:02.041
is deploy it against later
stage things that need

00:18:02.041 --> 00:18:05.291
a marketing arbitrage
to create victory.

00:18:05.291 --> 00:18:06.875
- [Ed] So,
you've basically said

00:18:06.875 --> 00:18:09.333
I'm gonna make these wild
investments, I'll do it

00:18:09.333 --> 00:18:11.625
in Series B because that's
where the funnel pinch

00:18:11.625 --> 00:18:12.792
works for me.

00:18:12.792 --> 00:18:14.458
- They'd feel a
lot less wild for me

00:18:14.458 --> 00:18:16.083
coming from angel

00:18:16.083 --> 00:18:19.333
A, so there's a lot more
data of an actual business

00:18:19.333 --> 00:18:22.333
and B, I can look at it
and say, okay her business

00:18:22.333 --> 00:18:23.375
she's got it all down

00:18:23.375 --> 00:18:27.500
her one opening or
opportunity or soft spot is

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.708
marketing arbitrage
the way I do it

00:18:29.708 --> 00:18:31.333
which I believe is best in class

00:18:31.333 --> 00:18:33.166
that's a good bet for
me 'cause I can bring

00:18:33.166 --> 00:18:35.792
the smartest money to the table.

00:18:35.792 --> 00:18:39.041
- [Ed] And so, it's not just 
that you've opened a barber shop

00:18:39.041 --> 00:18:42.125
to get paid to do haircuts
in a downturn, so that

00:18:42.125 --> 00:18:44.792
you don't lose all your venture
capital investible dollars,

00:18:44.792 --> 00:18:48.625
it's that there's a symbiosis
between the barber shop and--

00:18:48.625 --> 00:18:51.583
- Even further the reason
I am building VaynerMedia

00:18:51.583 --> 00:18:55.291
right now is long term,
I think it plays out for me

00:18:55.291 --> 00:18:58.208
on private equity
where I buy businesses

00:18:58.208 --> 00:19:00.250
at scale and run them
through the machine

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.708
as I call the VaynerMedia
and make that arbitrage.

00:19:03.708 --> 00:19:05.208
I took a step back seven
years ago and said,

00:19:05.208 --> 00:19:06.500
you know what, now that I'm
hanging out with all these

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:08.917
fancy people, you
characters and way fancier

00:19:08.917 --> 00:19:10.917
than you Travis, Sacca,
those kind of dudes,

00:19:10.917 --> 00:19:13.166
I'm like I don't look like
these dudes and chicks,

00:19:13.166 --> 00:19:15.875
I'm not these people,
like, here's what I am,

00:19:15.875 --> 00:19:19.708
I'm a marketer and let me
quadruple down at that skill,

00:19:19.708 --> 00:19:21.208
build infrastructure around that

00:19:21.208 --> 00:19:23.542
and deploy it against
this world I believe in

00:19:23.542 --> 00:19:26.166
so that's what I've
been focused on.

00:19:26.166 --> 00:19:29.000
- [Ed] And where
does wine fit into this?

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.875
- So wine fits into it in
a couple of different ways.

00:19:32.875 --> 00:19:35.375
I'll get very real and
transparent with you guys

00:19:35.375 --> 00:19:37.708
and you know, you have such
a unique perspective on this.

00:19:37.708 --> 00:19:39.917
My dad and I are
very different, you know,

00:19:39.917 --> 00:19:42.083
like as different as you
can really get, you know,

00:19:42.083 --> 00:19:46.375
personality-wise, viewing
the world, our backgrounds.

00:19:46.375 --> 00:19:48.041
As I was building
Wine Library and I was getting

00:19:48.041 --> 00:19:50.500
more attention and
things of that nature,

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:52.875
there was a real forming
tension for the first time

00:19:52.875 --> 00:19:55.166
in our relationship
because, as you know,

00:19:55.166 --> 00:19:56.959
my dad gave me
the greatest gift,

00:19:56.959 --> 00:19:59.542
I walked in as a 22-year-old
and he gave me free reign.

00:19:59.542 --> 00:20:02.125
I ran that business, every
decision, which is remarkable

00:20:02.125 --> 00:20:04.208
in hindsight,
especially now that I'm 40

00:20:04.208 --> 00:20:07.959
and he was 44 when he
allowed me to do that.

00:20:07.959 --> 00:20:10.792
But as the narrative changes,
the business changed,

00:20:10.792 --> 00:20:13.291
there was tension
between our relationship

00:20:13.291 --> 00:20:16.083
on, you know,
him disappearing from

00:20:16.083 --> 00:20:19.792
the narrative within
